Vibration window size still fixed & small
100 samples @ ~50 Hz = ~2 s → ok for slow vibrations, but
Too short for detecting slow trends (e.g. washing machine imbalance over 5–10 s)
No configurable duration (user might want 5 s or 10 s for appliance checks)
========================================================
Audio FFT is basic & CPU-heavy
Radix-2 in-place FFT on every 2048-sample block (~46 ms @ 44.1 kHz) is ok for flagship, but may stutter on budget devices
No windowing function (Hanning/Hamming) → spectral leakage → less accurate peaks
Only finds single dominant bin → poor for broadband or multi-tone sounds
===========================================================
dB still not true SPL
DB_OFFSET = 90.0 is a rough guess; real mic sensitivity varies 10–20 dB between phones
No user calibration mode (e.g. "quiet room → set as 30 dB")
==========================================================
No magnetometer / full rotation vector
Still yaw-unaware → limited to pitch/roll only (no compass heading)
Drift can accumulate over long periods despite complementary filter
==============================================================
No foreground service
App dies in background → no "always-ready" behaviour
Android ≥12 kills even foreground services without notification sometimes
***********************************************************************
Suggested improvements – next logical steps (early 2026)
*******************************************************************
Make vibration window configurable (top priority)
→ Add UI slider: 1 s / 3 s / 5 s / 10 s
→ Dynamically size ArrayDeque or switch to time-based pruning (better)
Improve FFT audio
→ Add Hanning window before FFT
→ Interpolate peak frequency (parabolic) for sub-bin accuracy
→ Optional: detect multiple strong peaks (e.g. harmonics)
Add user dB calibration
→ Button: "Quiet room" → average 5 s → set as baseline (e.g. 35 dB)
→ Relative offset stored in SharedPreferences
Consider TYPE_ROTATION_VECTOR or TYPE_GAME_ROTATION_VECTOR
→ Android fused orientation (usually better than manual complementary filter)
→ Gives full 3-axis (with magnetic heading if available)
Add foreground service skeleton
→ For any hope of background/always-on
→ Persistent notification: "Omni-Measure active"
Add basic vibration frequency detection
→ Simple FFT on linear accel window → detect dominant mechanical frequency (fan, pump)